research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.log:13918:items/thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral.md-56-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.log:13919:items/thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral.md-57-**Given:** A natural $m \ge 1$, reals $a \le b$, a function $f : [a,b] \to \mathbb{R}^{m}$ that is integrable when $a<b$, with components $f_0,\dots,f_{m-1}$, and the vector $v := \int_a^b f \in \mathbb{R}^{m}$; write $g(t) := \sum_{i<m} f_i(t)^{2}$, so that $\lVert f(t)\rVert_2 = \sqrt{g(t)}$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-p-norms-on-rn]]).
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.log:13920:items/thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral.md-58-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.log:13921:items/thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral.md:59:[L1] The vector-valued integral is componentwise: when $a<b$, $f$ is integrable exactly when every $f_i$ is bounded and Darboux integrable, and then $\bigl(\int_a^b f\bigr)_i = \int_a^b f_i$; $\int_a^a f = 0$ ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-darboux-integral]], [[def-oriented-integral]], [[def-bounded-set]]).
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.log:13922:items/thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral.md-60-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.log:13923:items/thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral.md-61-[L2] Linearity of the integral: integrable functions on $[a,b]$ are closed under sums and scalar multiples, and $\int_a^b(\lambda u + \mu w) = \lambda\int_a^b u + \mu\int_a^b w$ ([[thm-linearity-of-the-integral]]).
