research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-page-rn-as-a-normed-space.log:2776:research/audit/wave12-dispatch/certifier-a8-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.log:12727:items/thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral.md-34-and let $f : [a,b] \to \mathbb{R}^{m}$. If $a<b$, assume that $f$ is integrable
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-page-rn-as-a-normed-space.log:2777:research/audit/wave12-dispatch/certifier-a8-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.log:12742:items/thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral.md-57-**Given:** A natural $m \ge 1$, reals $a \le b$, a function $f : [a,b] \to \mathbb{R}^{m}$ that is integrable when $a<b$, with components $f_0,\dots,f_{m-1}$, and the vector $v := \int_a^b f \in \mathbb{R}^{m}$; write $g(t) := \sum_{i<m} f_i(t)^{2}$, so that $\lVert f(t)\rVert_2 = \sqrt{g(t)}$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-p-norms-on-rn]]).
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-page-rn-as-a-normed-space.log:2778:research/audit/wave12-dispatch/certifier-a8-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.log:12744:items/thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral.md:59:[L1] The vector-valued integral is componentwise: when $a<b$, $f$ is integrable exactly when every $f_i$ is bounded and Darboux integrable, and then $\bigl(\int_a^b f\bigr)_i = \int_a^b f_i$; $\int_a^a f = 0$ ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-darboux-integral]], [[def-oriented-integral]], [[def-bounded-set]]).
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-page-rn-as-a-normed-space.log:2801:research/audit/wave12-dispatch/certifier-a8-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.log:13805:- **Two routes to the mean value inequality, and why the other one was taken.** When $f'$ happens to be integrable, clause 1 together with [[thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral]] gives $$\lVert f(b)-f(a)\rVert_2 = \Bigl\lVert\int_a^b f'\Bigr\rVert_2 \le \int_a^b \lVert f'\rVert_2 \le M(b-a),$$ the last step by monotonicity of the integral. That is a second proof of [[thm-mean-value-inequality]] **under an extra hypothesis**. The theorem is proved the other way, from the scalar mean value theorem, precisely because it then needs no integrability at all: it applies to every differentiable $f$. The point is not academic — the companion page's witness for the failure of the equality form is differentiable and is nowhere assumed integrable.
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-page-rn-as-a-normed-space.log:2820:research/audit/wave12-dispatch/certifier-a8-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.log:14526:research/audit/wave12-judge.jsonl-72-{"id":"thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","keep":false,"reason":"The hypothesis 'f is integrable' is only defined for a<b in the cited definition, but the theorem allows a=b, making the premise ill-formed for a=b. The proof uses oriented integrals, but the definition does not define integrability on a singleton.","context_sha256":"b81430435077369e60146085dd9f57683ef37046d04b35c58268d854b2e3061d","item_sha256":"5e08ebb866f9c022762b859264b75ba3ef8b7cc4b189ac43f0997e0383e163f8","at":"2026-08-10T02:46:51.356Z"}
