research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-page-rn-as-a-normed-space.log:3592:[L6] $\lVert f(t)\rVert_2$ is integrable on $[0,1]$ and $\bigl\lVert\int_0^1 f\bigr\rVert_2 \le \int_0^1\lVert f\rVert_2$ ([[thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral]], [[def-norm-and-normed-space]]).
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-page-rn-as-a-normed-space.log:3628:- **Why the inequality is strict here.** Equality in [[thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral]] would require the integrand to point in a fixed direction, and $f(t) = (1,t,t^{2})$ changes direction as $t$ runs over $[0,1]$. That heuristic is not what is proved above: the proof separates the two sides by an explicit numerical bound, which is the only argument available at this point in the reading order.
